formal land tenure:It's a Partially,The government has three online databases to collect information on land tenure, two of which also serve as platforms for citizen services. These are located on closed servers. No online Land Book was found. Land rights are supposed to be published by land registry offices at the municipal level (bureaux de la publicité foncière), but these seem to be non-existent. The General Directorate for Land Tenure, Training and Rural Organization has a list of Certificates of Rural Land Ownership in all communes in Excel format offline. The homepage of the National Urban Land Development Agency includes ad hoc lists of people who won housing and commercial parcels developed and serviced by the Agency. There exists a searchable online cadastral map.;
communal land tenure:It's a No,No information was found. Registered pastoral areas are managed by the state.;
land tenure on indigenous people or ethnic minorities:It's a No,No information found. This type of data does not seem to be collected;
informal land parcels:It's a Partially,The government launched a digital platform to collect data on plots and homes purchased from ex-real estate developers and formalize these parcels. The platform was no longer accessible at the time of the assessment, potentially because data collection was complete.;
leasehold/concession rights:It's a No,No information found.

