formal land tenure:It's a Partially,The data on formal land tenure is mainly on communal land. No data on formal land tenure outside communal land was found.;
communal land tenure:It's a Yes,There is data on the number of certified and non-certified ejidos and agrarian communities, their area and the area to be divided between members within both ejidos and communities. There is also data on delimitation areas within ejidos and agrarian communities;
land tenure on indigenous people or ethnic minorities:It's a Yes,The indigenous population in Mexico generally live either in ejidos or in agrarian communities. So, the information in the links from the previous question apply to them. Nevertheless, the National Institute of Indigenous Peoples (INPI) provides open data on indigenous land (and more) searchable by people and by community. Data related to land is on the location of the indigenous peoples and in some cases the indigenous land area and how much is ejidos or agrarian communities. The database (catalogue) also includes information on afromexicans.;
informal land parcels:It's a Partially,There is data (number, areas) on ejidos and rural communities registered but without certification. There is also information about the number of beneficiaries of a program to regularize informal settlements. There is no data on informal land parcels outside ejidos and rural communities.;
leasehold/concession rights:It's a Yes,There is data on succession rights, a table containing the number of contracts for the alienation of land rights registered at the national and state level, and data on the number of transfers of inheritance rights carried out at the national and state level.

